ng a lesion or abnormality shouldn't be tagged SpecificDisease, even if parenthetical or aliased—it should be DiseaseClass if generic, otherwise untagged. The exception is when the span is itself a nosological name (eponym, proper-noun syndrome, or acronym), even modified, or when the alias supplies a standalone disease name.\n\nI'm checking edge cases like \"Coats telangiectasis,\" where the eponym \"Coats\" modifies the finding head noun \"telangiectasis\"—testing the head noun itself (disease/syndrome vs. finding/lesion) resolves the ambiguity consistently with the gold annotation leaving it untagged. This also doesn't conflict with how Modifier or CompositeMention are defined. I'll finalize this as a single Markdown bullet with no preamble, embedding IF/THEN/Does-not-apply structure concisely.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "- **Finding-Head Descriptors Are Not Named Diseases:** IF a candidate span's head noun is a descriptive term for a lesion, abnormality, sign, or mechanism (for example, a vessel-dilation, detachment, or deficiency noun modified by an anatomical or qualifying modifier), rather than a proper name, eponym, or acronym that designates the disorder as a whole, THEN do not assign SpecificDisease, even if the span appears inside a parenthetical gloss, an alias marker (\"so-called,\" \"also known as,\" \"i.e.\"), or a repeated mention. Assign DiseaseClass if the span names a generic condition category; otherwise leave it untagged. **This rule does NOT apply** when the head noun is itself a nosological term (a disease, syndrome, or condition name, or an acronym resolving to one), even when that term is preceded by an eponym or descriptive modifier (e.g., \"[Eponym] disease,\" \"[Eponym] syndrome\"). It also does not apply when the alias gloss introduces a standalone disease name rather than a description of a finding.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "e